// A top-level struct whose field names an instance whose own field names the
// allocator - a two-link chain, and the port used to break it at the first link.
//
// `Holder` is always emitted, so `Vec<int>` is reachable, so the `alloc` field
// inside that instance is reachable too. The port walked only the four
// non-generic prelude names, so the chain never started: the emitted C declared a
// `Vec_i32` whose allocator field had no declaration.
//
// Two sets do the work, because there are two questions. One answers "does the
// program MENTION this name" - which is what the four-name elision asks, and a
// user `enum Vec` that mentions nothing must not make the allocator look used.
// The other answers "can the emitted C REACH this type", which is what decides
// whether a declaration must be in the file. One set cannot be both strict and
// transitive, and it was doing both jobs.
struct Holder { b: *const Vec<int> }

fn main() { println(1); }
